== Where the atmospheric computation is spent

For each visible pixel, the view ray needs repeated density and light-path evaluations. A reusable GPU density-column table stores the geometric integrals of molecular and aerosol density along straight light paths. Since the table stores columns rather than color, changing the gas mixture or dust coefficients can reuse the same geometry and apply new extinction coefficients. Camera motion also leaves the shell geometry unchanged. The main atmosphere field normally uses quarter resolution; a depth-aware resolve reconstructs smooth regions and reintegrates full-resolution pixels near discontinuities. Terrain, stars, and the Sun stay at full resolution. This matches the physical structure of the calculation: smooth optical fields tolerate coarse sampling, while silhouettes and depth edges do not. @fig:atmos-pipeline summarizes the pipeline.

An alternative is full-resolution ray integration for every pixel. It avoids resolve artifacts, but repeats nearly identical work across neighboring sky pixels. A larger multidimensional lookup could reduce per-pixel work further, but would be harder to update for changing aerosol and humidity states. The current split keeps the expensive geometric column reusable while leaving composition as a live control.

#figure(image("figures/atmosphere/pipeline.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Atmospheric rendering graph. A reusable density-column lookup and a reduced scattering field lower repeated work; full-resolution depth controls edge reconstruction. Generated schematic.]) <fig:atmos-pipeline>

The project's recorded Quadro M1000M benchmark at 1280×720 reported 208 ms mean frame time for the original full-field method, 138 ms with the column lookup, and 26 ms with reduced fields and shared ray calculations. These were 90-frame measurements of one surface scene with Debug CPU builds, recorded in the README; @fig:perf reproduces them rather than claiming a new experiment. The approximately eightfold change is specific to that workload and GPU. A separate RelWithDebInfo run reported 28.5 ms. A headless software-rendered capture is not comparable to this hardware benchmark.

#figure(image("figures/atmosphere/performance.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Reported mean frame times from `README.md`, Quadro M1000M, 1280×720, 90 frames. These bars visualize previously reported measurements, not a new benchmark.]) <fig:perf>

A profiler now records CPU stage times and asynchronous GPU timestamp results without blocking for each query. CPU frame time, GPU pass time, and swap/presentation delay answer different questions; adding CPU and GPU times would double-count overlap. A completed HDR scene may be reused if camera, time, viewport, geometry, and pending-terrain state match. The HUD remains separately drawn so input can reveal or hide it immediately.

= Visibility, exposure, and interactive constraints

== Diagnostics, dependency boundaries and adaptive quality

The `I`/Tab panel reports timings and optional Linux NVIDIA NVML utilization. Utilization is sampled off the render thread and is device-wide, while GPU pass milliseconds belong to this application's measured passes. Software rendering and ambiguous GPU matching show `N/A`; a fake driver library tests the ABI path without claiming validation on NVIDIA hardware. @fig:overlay is a real captured panel.

#figure(image("../screenshots/performance-overlay.png", width: 83%), caption: [Actual current performance overlay under Xvfb/Mesa llvmpipe. The white-bordered black panel reports FPS, frame time, GPU pass time, and utilization availability; its numbers are capture-environment observations.]) <fig:overlay>

A best-effort 20 FPS quality target uses a smoothed frame estimate $m_t = 0.85 m_(t-1) + 0.15 f_t$ to select one of six scene-resolution scales, from 100% down to 25%. After a 1.5-second cooldown, sustained times above 55 ms lower resolution; times below 32 ms permit recovery. Only moving interactive scenes feed the controller. A graphics-memory estimate can cap the initial scale, reserving most reported memory for other uses; fixed-resolution captures remain deterministic. The HUD stays at native window resolution. @fig:adaptive shows the control law, not a measured response trace. This is a rendering control, not a change to the atmosphere's physical parameters. Its lower resolution may still lose thin clouds or distant silhouettes; a strict 20 FPS guarantee is impossible once the minimum scale is reached or scene cost is unrelated to pixel count.

#figure(image("figures/interaction/adaptive.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Actual controller thresholds and discrete scene scales; bar heights are relative scale, not measured frame rates. Generated schematic.]) <fig:adaptive>

== Visibility must follow the light path

The same visibility principle applies to air and water. A mountain blocks direct sunlight from nearby air, so the direct in-scattering term uses the planet's terrain depth map. The finite solar disk's twilight region remains, as does the approximate indirect fill. For water, a valid bright sample in the reflection image does not prove that a water point is lit: that point may lie on the night side or behind terrain. The direct reflection term therefore requires positive local Sun incidence and terrain visibility. Indirect water radiance remains. @fig:ocean illustrates the distinction. The gallery's terrain-shadow and twilight cases are controlled visual regressions.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/ocean.svg", width: 100%), caption: [A reflected bright sample can exist in image space even when the water point lies on the night side. Local incidence and shadow-map visibility gate the direct reflection term. Generated schematic.]) <fig:ocean>

#figure(grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 8pt,
  image("../screenshots/terrain-shadows.png", width: 100%),
