== Interpreting motion from the camera

Ten-revolution predictive trails and body cards help distinguish local orbits from inertial paths. The path color averages terrain vertex RGB factors multiplied by the body's configured base tint; labels report name, parent, axes, eccentricity, period, radius, and mass. Sampling the entire orbital system, rather than each child's local ellipse alone, is necessary for moving-parent trails. The overlay is toggled by `O` and hidden in planet and surface cameras; real X11 input tests verify both behaviors.

Orbit-to-surface entry is a one-second wall-clock transition. The starting pose is the last actually rendered view, avoiding a first-frame jump after the key event. An eased parameter $q(u)=3u^2-2u^3$ drives spherical interpolation of the planet-relative radial direction, logarithmic interpolation of radius, quaternion interpolation of orientation, and field-of-view interpolation. Intermediate positions are clamped above sampled terrain and water. The target is resampled while the planet moves, so the transition ends at the current surface pose. This is a camera path, not a physically accelerated descent; a dynamics-based flight model would require forces, velocities, and collision response. @fig:transition shows the path and ease curve.

#figure(image("figures/interaction/transition.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Orbit-to-surface descent. The curve shows the implemented smoothstep parameter; the spatial path is schematic because the actual start, target, and terrain vary by scene.]) <fig:transition>

= Evaluation and model boundaries

== What the evidence establishes

The repository contains 39 CTest entries covering configuration and orbital invariants; terrain and camera behavior; lighting, shadows, atmosphere and refraction on OpenGL; renderer captures; adaptive quality; and real GLFW input under Xvfb. The 12 README images are checked against their SHA-256 manifest. These checks establish specific numerical and rendered behaviors on the tested software renderer. They do not establish physical fidelity, portability to every GPU driver, or a guaranteed 20 FPS for arbitrary scenes.

The model boundaries are deliberate. Orbits are prescribed two-body ellipses with hierarchical recoil. Terrain is a deterministic synthetic height field with bounded tessellation. Atmospheric scale heights are chosen relative to shell size; the model does not solve hydrostatic temperature profiles, cloud dynamics, weather, or multiple scattering. Refraction bends view rays and reprojects available distant imagery but keeps near opaque geometry and direct shadow rays straight. Atmospheric temperature is read from JSON rather than evolved from sunlight and orbit. These limits define what a visual comparison can support.

== Rebuilding the paper and the evidence

From the repository root, regenerate the deterministic vector figures and compile the Typst source:

```sh
python3 docs/journal/make_figures.py
typst compile --root . docs/journal/paper.typ docs/journal/paper.pdf
```

The 12 SVGs require only Python's standard library. The paper references the versioned PNGs already stored in `docs/screenshots/`. To rerun the application tests in a headless OpenGL environment, build the CMake project with testing enabled and execute `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 xvfb-run -a ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure`. Exact image-generation commands and checksums are in `docs/captures/generation.json`.

= Conclusion

The renderer's most useful physical separation is between the state of a world and the approximation used to draw it. A fixed terrain height field can be sampled at changing mesh densities; prescribed orbital positions can be evaluated at any time; and atmospheric extinction, in-scattering, and refractive curvature can be varied from a configured air state. The atmosphere is strongest as an interactive optical explanation of why density, path length, particle type, and visibility change a sky. Its limits are equally specific: shell-relative scale heights, RGB coefficients, single scattering, fixed reference weather, and image-based refraction prevent a predictive climate or precision optical interpretation.

= References and implementation map

R1. NASA, _Basics of Space Flight, Chapter 3: Gravity and Mechanics_, #link("https://science.nasa.gov/learn/basics-of-space-flight/chapter3-3/")[official orbit primer]. Used for the classical two-body context; the implementation details above come from `src/simulation/OrbitalSystem.h`.

R2. E. Bruneton and F. Neyret, “Precomputed Atmospheric Scattering,” _Computer Graphics Forum_ 27(4), 2008, #link("https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-8659.2008.01245.x")[doi:10.1111/j.1467-8659.2008.01245.x]. The project uses a smaller runtime single-scattering approximation and a limited reusable lookup.

R3. NIST Engineering Metrology Toolbox, #link("https://emtoolbox.nist.gov/wavelength/documentation.asp")[Index of Refraction of Air documentation]. Background for pressure, temperature, humidity and refractive index; this project does not claim NIST-grade refractometry.

R4. IAU 2015 resolutions, #link("https://www.iau.org/static/resolutions/IAU2015_English.pdf")[official PDF]. The rendering code uses the Resolution B2 bolometric zero-point convention for luminosity.

R5. _PlanetSimulation_ repository: implementation in `src/`, shaders in `shaders/`, checks in `tests/`, and captured images with reproducibility metadata in `docs/captures/generation.json`.

R6. NASA Glenn Research Center, #link("https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/Numbers/Math/Mathematical_Thinking/ideal_gases_under_constant.htm")[Ideal Gases under Constant Volume, Constant Pressure, Constant Temperature, and Adiabatic Conditions]. The hydrostatic, isothermal scale-height derivation is the physical motivation for the exponential profile, not the formula used to set the renderer's shell-relative scale heights.

